  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nCurrent radar reflectivity depicts a large area of stratiform rain \nacross MN and western WI, which is associated with a shortwave \ntrough moving across the upper Midwest. As seen in visible satellite \nimagery, overcast skies are present across the western CWA, with \ncloud cover breaking up into mostly to partly cloudy skies in the \ncentral and eastern U.P.\n\nA generally zonal 500mb pattern has set up across the Great Lakes \nthis afternoon due to the presence of a deep low pressure center \nover northeastern Quebec and Newfoundland and a broad 596 dam over \nTX, OK, and AR. Deep troughs are present on either side of this area \nof high pressure over northern NV and eastern FL, respectively. A \nridge-riding shortwave continues to make its way across the northern \nMidwest, bringing the aforementioned precipitation to MN and WI. \nThis stratiform rain is expected to continue progressing across the \nsouthern portion of our CWA tonight through Saturday evening. \nAccumulation probabilities remain low, with the 12z HREF indicating \na less than 10% chance for 24-hour accumulations to exceed a third \nof an inch anywhere in the CWA outside of portions of southern \nMenominee county. The 00z LREF continues to be a little more \noptimistic, with a 10-20% chance of exceeding a third of an inch for \nareas south of US-2. In either case, areas outside of the southern \nU.P. are expected to remain mostly dry. With light winds, humid \nconditions, and minimum temperatures in the upper 30s to low 40s, \nthere is a slight chance (10-25%) that some frost formation could \noccur in the inland regions of the eastern U.P. and central western \nU.P. tonight. Those with sensitive outdoor vegetation may need to \nconsider taking protective action to prevent frost damage.\n\n\nThe trough associated with the Quebec low progresses eastward on \nSunday while a 500mb ridge is expected to build across the northern \nPlains and south-central Canada. South of the ridge, across the \ncentral and southern Plains, a trough is expected to deepen, \ncreating a split flow pattern. The U.P. is expected to remain under \nthe northern ridge through Monday morning, keeping the area dry with \nseasonably cool temperatures. Uncertainty increases toward the \nmiddle of the week, as ensemble spread increase. However, the latest \nCPC temperature and precipitation outlooks over next the 14 days \nindicate warmer and possibly drier than normal conditions.",
